  "u5-ns1": passage("u5-ns1", "Enlightenment ideas and reform movements",
    "Enlightenment writers of the 1600s and 1700s argued that reason and observation, rather than tradition or religious authority alone, should guide how people understood nature and society. John Locke wrote that people have natural rights to life, liberty, and property and that government rests on the consent of the governed. Montesquieu argued that liberty was safest when legislative, executive, and judicial power were held by different bodies. Rousseau wrote of a social contract in which the people as a whole were sovereign. Voltaire attacked religious intolerance and the power of the clergy, and some thinkers became deists, who believed that God had created the universe but did not intervene in it. These ideas spread through printed books, newspapers, salons, and coffeehouses, and later reformers used them to argue against practices that their authors had sometimes accepted. Abolitionists in Britain won a ban on the British slave trade in 1807 and on slavery in most of the British Empire in 1833. Activists for women's rights pointed out that the language of natural rights rarely included women, and campaigns for women's suffrage grew in the later 1800s."),
  "u5-ns2": passage("u5-ns2", "Nations and empires in the 1800s",
    "Nationalism, the idea that people who share a language, history, and culture should form their own state, reshaped Europe in the 1800s. In Italy, Count Cavour, prime minister of Piedmont-Sardinia, used diplomacy and war against Austria, while Giuseppe Garibaldi's volunteers conquered the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies; a Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861. In the German states, the Prussian minister Otto von Bismarck won three short wars, against Denmark, Austria, and France, and the German Empire was proclaimed in 1871. Nationalism threatened empires that ruled many peoples. Greeks won independence from the Ottoman Empire in 1830, and Serbia, Romania, and Bulgaria later broke away. The Austrian Empire divided power with Hungarian leaders in 1867, creating the Dual Monarchy of Austria-Hungary. Nationalism also spread beyond Europe. In the Philippines, the writer José Rizal criticized Spanish rule in his novels, and Spanish authorities executed him in 1896. In Europe, Theodor Herzl argued in 1896 that Jews, facing persecution, needed a state of their own, and he organized the first Zionist Congress in 1897."),
  "u5-ns3": passage("u5-ns3", "Machines, mills, and coal in Britain",
    "In the middle 1700s, most British cloth was spun and woven by hand in rural homes, often by families working for merchants who supplied raw materials and bought the finished goods. A series of inventions changed cotton spinning. James Hargreaves's spinning jenny (about 1764) let one worker spin several threads at once. Richard Arkwright's water frame (1769) used water power to spin strong thread, and in 1771 Arkwright built a mill at Cromford that brought hundreds of workers under one roof and set their hours by the machines. Samuel Crompton's spinning mule (1779) combined features of both. Later improvements to the steam engine, especially James Watt's design of the 1770s, let mills run on coal instead of falling water, so factories could be built in towns near coalfields and ports. Britain had large coal deposits close to the surface, and coal also fueled new ways to make iron. Cotton imports grew rapidly, much of the cotton coming from plantations in the southern United States worked by enslaved people."),
  "u5-ns4": passage("u5-ns4", "Industry spreads unevenly",
    "Industrialization spread from Britain in the 1800s, though not evenly. Belgium, rich in coal and iron, built factories and railways early in the century. In the German lands, industry grew quickly after the 1850s, especially in the coal and iron region of the Ruhr, where firms such as Krupp made steel, rails, and cannons. The northeastern United States built textile mills and, after the Civil War, became a leading producer of steel and oil. Russia and Japan began to industrialize late in the century. Elsewhere, older industries suffered. In India, cotton cloth made by hand had long been sold across Asia, Africa, and Europe. In the 1800s, cheaper machine-made British cotton cloth flooded Indian markets, the number of Indian handloom weavers fell, and India exported more raw cotton to Britain instead."),
  "u5-ns5": passage("u5-ns5", "Wires, steam, and cold storage",
    "New technologies of the 1800s made the world smaller. Samuel Morse sent the first long-distance telegraph message in the United States in 1844, and telegraph lines soon ran along railways in many countries. A lasting cable across the Atlantic began working in 1866, and by the early 1870s cables linked Britain to India and Australia, so news and orders that once took months took hours. Steamships, at first used on rivers, grew larger and more efficient with iron and then steel hulls, screw propellers, and better engines, and they began to replace sailing ships on long routes after the Suez Canal opened in 1869. In 1882 the ship Dunedin carried a cargo of frozen meat from New Zealand to Britain, and refrigerated ships soon brought meat from Argentina, Australia, and New Zealand to European markets."),
  "u5-ns6": passage("u5-ns6", "Industrializing from above",
    "Some governments tried to promote industry themselves, often to defend against stronger foreign powers. In Egypt, Muhammad Ali, the Ottoman governor from 1805 to 1848, built state-owned textile mills and arsenals, sent students to study in France, and required farmers to grow cotton for export; many of his factories closed after Britain forced him to cut his army and to open Egypt to British goods. In China, officials of the Self-Strengthening Movement founded arsenals and shipyards in the 1860s, such as the Jiangnan Arsenal in Shanghai, but they hoped to adopt Western weapons while keeping Confucian government unchanged. After 1868 Japan's Meiji government built railways and telegraph lines, set up model factories such as the Tomioka silk mill (1872), and later sold many state firms to private companies. In Russia, the finance minister Sergei Witte raised tariffs, borrowed from foreign investors, and pushed the building of the Trans-Siberian Railway, begun in 1891."),
  "u5-ns7": passage("u5-ns7", "New ways of doing business",
    "Industrial capitalism changed how businesses raised money and how governments regulated trade. In Britain, laws of 1855 and 1856 let companies form with limited liability: if a company failed, its shareholders lost only what they had invested, not their homes and other property. Joint-stock companies could now raise money from many small investors, and stock exchanges grew in London, New York, and other cities. Many economists argued for free trade, and in 1846 Britain repealed the Corn Laws, which had taxed imported grain. In the United States, a few very large firms came to control whole industries; John D. Rockefeller's Standard Oil controlled most of the country's oil refining by the 1880s. Some companies operated across borders. The Boston-based United Fruit Company, formed in 1899, owned banana plantations and railways in Central America and the Caribbean and shipped bananas to markets in the United States."),
